# PC-S51-A concise pagination revision — scratch proposal

Status: unbuilt planning/draft only. No canonical source, metadata, installed
artifact, accepted v1 record, or run state has been changed. The historical
v1 acceptance remains true of its exact bytes. Adoption belongs to fresh
proper-study v3 packets after the coordinator releases the reviewed contract.

## Proposed result

Aim for 11 physical pages, with 10–12 the authorized range. Keep the existing
comfortable Palatino body measure and approximately 11-point text on 15-point
leading. Use a compact celebration title, meaningful headings, ordinary
paragraphs and restrained tabular matter. Do not enlarge type, insert blank
space, reproduce protected liturgical bodies, or repeat lane summaries to
obtain pages.

The existing expansive study is 20 pages and already has three whole-proper
lanes, each with multiple substantive authors and its own four senses. Preserve
those arguments and all twelve lane distillations. Its necessary new chronology
home is a terminal appendix using the same chronology component as the concise
study; that may make the study about 21 pages, comfortably inside 20–50. This is
a forecast, not an inspected page count. The 1,288-word homily needs fresh
derivation/review under the new run but no substantive change is currently
identified.

## Physical reader sequence and budget

| Page | Content and treatment | Draft/budget |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Compact celebration/Year A title; complete two-column appointed map in ritual order; one unheaded table of exactly Literal, Allegorical, Moral, Anagogical | About 300–370 words including table labels; ten ritual rows cover all eleven keys, with both Communion alternatives in one row |
| 2 | Sole `Scriptural Date and Location`; eight canonical-order dossiers, generated chronology, local setting distinctions | About 400–550 words including all date alternatives; actual annotation projection determines fit |
| 3–4 | `The Propers: Themes and Movement` exactly two substantive pages; four developed functional units, one opening heading, no continuation heading | Draft 796 substantive words; about 400/page |
| 5–10 | Developed comparison begins on page 5 as `The Propers: Detailed Commentary`; five question/relationship units interleave the three readings | Draft 2,589 substantive words; target about six pages with meaningful paragraph and heading rhythm |
| 10–12, as needed | Compact work-wide scope, source notes/references and generation furniture follow the completed argument | Retain existing bounded apparatus, add only actual chronology authorities/limits; target last page 11 |

The current two prose draft components total 3,385 substantive words by the
same Pandoc AST method used for the accepted concise author's count: omit
headings, notes and tables, then count whitespace-delimited plain prose. The
opening diagram remains prose in that method. Page-one tables, chronology,
references and terminal metadata are excluded. See `word-counts.json`.
This is about 51% of the accepted study's 6,608 substantive words, compared
with the historical concise count of 1,750. Actual build and review must decide
pagination, readability and redundancy; no PDF has been built in this task.

## Four-page opening

`components/01-inventory.tex` has ten ritual slots and all eleven elements:
Entrance, Collect, Isaiah, Psalm, Philippians, adapted Acts acclamation,
Matthew, Offerings, both mutually exclusive Communions, After Communion. The
map uses locators and existing incipits, not ICEL/CCD bodies. Open chant,
Preface and Eucharistic Prayer selections remain unselected; their scope
belongs in the terminal apparatus rather than invented page-one appointments.

`components/02-overview.tex` contains exactly four overview rows. It compresses
the accepted arguments without replacing the expansive study's lane-specific
four senses. The first page ends after this table.

`components/03-chronology.tex` is a layout/interface draft, not a generated
chronology file. It imports the future canonical generated annotations once
and uses `\chronodate{key}{\chronologyannotation{key}}`. It must be adopted only
after the adapter and fresh research own the real record. Dossier order is
Psalms 37, 119, 145; Isaiah 55; Matthew 20; John 10; Acts 16; Philippians 1.
That is canonical book/verse order, not ritual order. The same-edition older
pilot is a layout reference only: its date prose and its Isaiah-before-Psalms
ordering are not inherited as authority.

Page two is feasible but is the principal unproved fit risk. The default
corpus returns six disputed Matthew composition dates, two John dates, two
Philippians dates, and both Acts composition and two mission-event dates.
Three psalm dossiers share a broad composition boundary. Preserve all
applicable assertions and their relations through the generated concise
projection. Do not manually shorten raw dates, suppress alternatives, use
tiny type, or date the parable's fictional workday as an event. Start near
9-point table text, modest cell padding and a wider date column; use compact
full-width explanatory rows and remove redundant dossier prose before
considering any typography adjustment. If the projected annotations cannot
fit legibly, report that concrete result to the coordinator; do not silently
spill onto page three or omit a claim.

`components/04-themes.tex` has four functional units: help teaches the heart;
mercy changes the measure of justice; an opened heart enters service; the
received gift becomes a common life. The first two occupy page three, the
last two page four. Every unit is integrated, with both Communion endings
conditional. Isaiah/Gospel correlation, the Psalm's response and the
semi-continuous Epistle remain distinct. The draft closes page four explicitly
before commentary. Future proofing should tune prose distribution rather than
stretch spacing; the current draft is approximately 400 substantive words per
page and makes no claim of already satisfying the physical gate.

## Commentary expansion

`components/05-interleaved-commentary.tex` preserves the existing concise
argument and adds approximately 950 words within its comparison points:

1. The personal vineyard and the Church across history: how Augustine's
   cultivated hearts join the two scales without harmonizing Gregory's and
   Augustine's different hour allocations.
2. The distorted eye in Psalm 37 and Matthew: distinguish evildoers' apparent
   success from mercy granted to called late workers; retain contractual
   justice rather than treating the parable as a wage-policy rule.
3. Earlier workers and waiting: honor Israel's holy fathers within Gregory's
   vineyard and compare his Christological explanation with Augustine's
   common resurrection and Chrysostom's narrative explanation.
4. Lydia's active reception: compare divine initiative, attention and
   hospitality without converting Chrysostom into an Augustinian formula or
   Augustine into a theory of unaided initiative.
5. Paul's costly remaining: develop Chrysostom's trader/athlete/soldier
   imagery and Aquinas's friendship argument so service is a substantive
   alternative to resentment.
6. The communal prayer and the purchased flock: compare the Psalm's body
   voice with the Shepherd's personal center, then bring the three readings
   together through their distinct answers to labor, despair and possession.

No new patristic or liturgical claim is needed for these additions; each has
an explicit home in the accepted study and research. `source-map.md` records
those homes. The future author should verify actual cited evidence afresh,
retain exact source notes and rights limits, and remove any genuine overlap
between themes and commentary discovered in the proof. The thematic pages
state the movement; the later pages earn the detail through comparative
reasoning, distinctions and objections.

## New evidence and workflow boundary

Chronology is the substantive new evidence dependency. The present CLI
adapter was actually tested and refused the postconciliar ID. Root assigned
the bounded adapter repair. Its proposed input lives at
`.scratch/postconciliar-chronology/chronology-inputs.toml`; canonical adoption,
generated `research/chronology.toml` and `chronology-annotations.tex`, source
ancestry/dependency pins, full-study appendix and references all belong to
fresh v3 research/authoring and cold review. No generated chronology is
handwritten in this plan.

Read-only direct queries were made for all 38 relevant canonical verses and
retained under `chronology/`, with actual CLI commands and exact payload
hashes in `chronology/manifest.json`. These are planning evidence, not a
replacement per-proper source owner. Whole-verse queries must not silently
widen the Lectionary's `20c`, `27a`, `14b` or `16a` cuts. The adapter's explicit
numbering/citation mapping must govern the final element-level intersection.

Existing rights/retention limits remain: no complete 2008 Latin or U.S. 2011
altar-book collation is newly claimed; Jerome's digital printing remains
unidentified and remote-only; Irenaeus OCR is not promoted into facsimile
evidence; the abridged Augustine Psalm witness is not silently equated with
the fuller Latin. No extra direct quotation is required.

## Contract and later verification

Use the coordinator's additive `presentation_contract =
"interpretive-pagination-v1"` with separate inventory, overview, chronology
and themes `front-matter` components and synthesis-only `integrated`
commentary. See the scratch `presentation-fragment.toml`. Add `zref-user` and
`zref-abspage` in the actual entrypoint and use the physical labels supplied
by the new owning contract. Do not change frozen v1 artifacts or restamp
their acceptance.

After the tested v3 contract is committed and the coordinator releases a new
seed, follow each real emitted packet: new chronology research and cold
review, study review, concise authoring/preflight/cold review, homily review,
settled three-PDF builds, every-page visual review, sole canonical web review,
exact installs and root-controlled publication wiring/gates. A requested
10–12-page result requires actual physical-page checks, not this word budget.
